//! The op numbers (spec §2, §5.7): the three universal ops and every
//! per-object op table.
//!
//! This is the half of the module the vDSO discipline is about: userspace never
//! writes one of these, which is what leaves the kernel free to renumber
//! between builds.
//!
//! The argument encodings ride along at the end, under the last op-table
//! banner: `ClockType`, `MapAccess` and its mask, `EventMode`, and the pipe
//! body and handle-slot dimensions with their packed meta. They are not op
//! numbers; the facade's header says which of them `sos` republishes to
//! userspace and why.
//!
//! Reached through the `sosabi` facade; see `lib.saw`.

// =============================================================================
// The universal ops
// =============================================================================
//
// Each is an act that is the same for every object kind, so each is one number
// rather than one enum case per kind, and all are intercepted at the one point
// in the dispatch that knows a handle's entry and not yet its kind. They
// descend from the top of the number space so that the per-kind tables, dense
// from zero, can never grow into them.

/// RELEASE: destroy the caller's handle, whatever kind it names.
///
/// It is one number and not one case per kind, because release is the same act
/// for every kind: unbind the entry, bump the slot's generation, and drop the
/// entry's reference on the object. A per-kind case would be the same code
/// written once per kind with as many ways to drift, and the dispatch would
/// have to reach the kind before it could see the op, one step later than the
/// only step that matters here.
///
/// What the dropped reference frees depends on the kind. System and Clock
/// handles are not counted, so releasing one frees nothing. A counted object
/// frees at its last reference, with these exceptions: a live Process keeps
/// running (only a `Gone` process's record is reclaimed), a Thread that has not
/// exited keeps running in its slot (it frees once it has exited or been
/// invalidated and nothing names it), a Memory region stays while an installed
/// mapping row still grants its bytes or, on a pool root, while pieces of it
/// are on loan, and a pipe connection or one-shot exchange frees only once both
/// of its halves are gone and nothing in flight remains: a connection lives
/// while any of its exchanges does, and a staged exchange settles only once its
/// connection's outlet is gone.
///
/// `0xFFFF` is outside every per-object table, and that is a rule rather than a
/// coincidence. Every op table in this file is dense from zero and stays that
/// way, so no kind can ever grow into this number. A table that wanted 65535
/// ops would have to say so here first. `from(raw:)` on a per-kind enum
/// therefore never sees this value: the dispatch intercepts it before the kind
/// match.
///
/// It is ungated. No right is checked, on any kind. Destroying your own
/// capability instance harms nobody: identity lives in the object's slot, not
/// in the handle, so a released handle takes nothing away from anyone else
/// holding one. Releasing your System handle or your own Process handle is
/// legal but doomed (the sandboxed-compute precedent), and losing the authority
/// is what the caller asked for. If a gate is ever wanted it is a new universal
/// bit, never a retasking of an existing one.
///
/// Releasing a word that does not resolve (`NO_HANDLE`, a malformed word, a
/// stale one whose generation has moved, or a second release of the same word)
/// is the ordinary `FaultReason.BadHandle`. Using a handle you released is
/// broken code, which is the line the generations exist to draw.
///
/// Close is not this op and is not built. Release destroys your handle; close
/// would end the object for everyone, would exist only on kinds with an
/// end-state, and would arrive under a per-kind right.
public static RELEASE_OP: UInt = 0xFFFF

/// MINT: produce a sibling handle onto the same object, in the caller's own
/// table, with rights the caller may only narrow.
///
/// It is universal for release's reason. Naming an object twice is the same act
/// for every kind (copy the entry, narrow the rights, bind the copy), so it is
/// one number the dispatch intercepts before it has decided the kind. `0xFFFE`
/// is the next number down from `RELEASE_OP` for the reason that one is at the
/// top: every per-object table is dense from zero and stays below both, which
/// the `static_assert`s below hold.
///
/// The argument is a keep mask: `new = held & rights`, and all-ones is "the
/// same rights the source has", which is what the no-argument `mint()` spelling
/// means. Three things fall out of that one decision:
///
///   - It needs no introspection. A caller names the rights it wants the
///     sibling to have, and the intersection quietly drops anything the source
///     did not hold, so the mask works without the caller knowing what the
///     source carries. `INFO_OP` answers that question when a caller wants it
///     for its own reasons.
///   - It is total. Naming a right the source never had is a no-op, so there is
///     no over-ask to fault on. One policy mask therefore applies to handles of
///     several kinds without the launcher knowing which bits each carries.
///   - No-amplification is structural (§3). The result is a subset of the
///     source by construction, not by a check that could be forgotten, so the
///     op cannot widen a handle even if the mask is garbage.
///
/// A keep mask fails closed, and that is the reason for the polarity. When a
/// kind gains a right later, a mask written before that right existed is a
/// whitelist that has never heard of it and therefore denies it. A removal mask
/// fails the other way: an old mask would pass a new authority through
/// silently, and a launcher masking a System handle for a child would hand
/// every future System capability to every child it had ever configured.
/// Failing closed costs an audit when a right is added; failing open costs a
/// quiet escalation, and nobody is looking.
///
/// Subtractive intent stays available as notation rather than as semantics:
/// `mint(rights: ~(Transfer as UInt32) & ALL)` is "everything except Transfer",
/// spelled so that the fail-open choice is visible at the call site instead of
/// being the language of the op. A launcher that means a whitelist writes one,
/// and a launcher that means a complement writes the complement.
///
/// It is gated on the universal `Mint` bit (§3's bit 1). A source without it is
/// an `AccessDenied` fault, and since `Mint` is itself a bit a mask can remove,
/// a launcher hands out a sibling that cannot mint again and proliferation
/// stops where it says. A source that does not resolve is the ordinary
/// `BadHandle`, as for release.
///
/// The sibling is independent: its own table slot, its own generation, its own
/// release. It names the same object, so it is a second name rather than a
/// second object.
public static MINT_OP: UInt = 0xFFFE

/// INFO: read facts about the caller's own handle, whatever kind it names.
///
/// `arg0` is a `HandleInfo` selector and the op answers that one value: a
/// syscall carries one status and one value, and a rights word can use all 32
/// bits, so the rights and the kind cannot share a word. An `arg0` that is not
/// a `HandleInfo` is a `FaultReason.BadArg` fault. The selector set has room
/// for more facts.
///
/// It is universal for release's reason: describing a handle is the same act
/// for every kind, so the dispatch intercepts it before it has decided the
/// kind. `0xFFFD` is the next number down from `MINT_OP`, and every per-object
/// table stays below it, which the `static_assert`s below hold.
///
/// It is ungated. No right is checked, on any kind and with no universal bit
/// spent. It describes the caller's own handle, not the object behind it, and
/// every op on the object is still checked on use. It is what makes "a missing
/// right is a caller-checkable fault" checkable for a received handle, whose
/// rights the sender chose.
///
/// It answers normally on a handle to an invalid object (spec §2.10): the
/// handle and its rights still exist, and `Revoked` belongs to ops on the
/// object. A word that does not resolve (`NO_HANDLE`, a malformed word, a
/// stale one whose generation has moved, a released one) is the ordinary
/// `FaultReason.BadHandle`.
public static INFO_OP: UInt = 0xFFFD

// =============================================================================
// The System object's op table (spec §2)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on the System object, not a global syscall number. Another
/// object type numbers its own ops from zero, and the pair (handle, op) is what
/// selects an implementation.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the op arrives in (a7), because
/// that value is process-controlled. A narrower backing would need the kernel
/// to truncate a7 before `from(raw:)` could look at it, and `0x100` would then
/// arrive as a valid `DebugPrint`: a hostile process picking an op it was
/// refused. At register width the conversion is exact and `from(raw:)` rejects
/// every number that is not an op, so the range check and the decode are one
/// operation instead of two that could disagree.
///
/// `ProcessSelf` takes a keep mask, on `MINT_OP`'s terms: `process_rights() &
/// keep`, all-ones is the full default set, and a bit the default set does not
/// hold is a no-op. The mask is the deriving process's own argument, so only
/// the deriving process can narrow the Process handle it derives, and it can
/// derive a wider one on its next call. A launcher's mask on the System
/// sibling it gave a child does not reach the handle the child derives, and a
/// launcher has no way to narrow it (spec §9).
///
/// `ClockGet` is a getter rather than a factory: the object already exists (one
/// kernel-eternal Clock per domain), so the op only hands out a handle onto it.
/// Every ask mints a fresh one, as `ProcessSelf` does: a handle is a capability
/// instance, independently held and independently released, which is what an
/// owning wrapper needs to be an owner of. What §3 forbids is amplification,
/// not two names: a mint always carries the kind's default rights and the
/// minting itself is gated (`ClockGet`, `ProcessSelf`), so asking twice gets a
/// caller nothing it could not have got once. The op is consequently fallible
/// on repetition (a full handle table is `NoResource`, a caller at its handle
/// quota `QuotaExceeded`).
///
/// `ProcessCreate` is the factory for processes, and `InterruptBind` (below)
/// the one for Interrupts: processes are minted by the System, while
/// process-scoped objects are minted by your Process. A
/// process table is machine-global and bounded by machine facts, so a refusal
/// to create one is a System answer, while threads, events, waiters and timers
/// stay their process's own. `ProcessSelf` is the precedent: a Process handle
/// has always been something you ask the System object for. It is gated on
/// `SystemRight.ProcessCreate`; its arguments are two Memory handles, which
/// spend no right of their own.
///
/// `SlabDonate` is the op that changes what the machine can hold rather than
/// what the caller holds. It takes a `SlabKind` and a Memory handle and
/// consumes the capability, and no op returns the donated range to a pool. It
/// is on System for `ProcessCreate`'s reason: a slab is a machine-global
/// resource, so the op that sizes one is a System answer. Its second argument
/// is a Memory handle spending no `MemoryRight` of its own (the operation's
/// argument, not a second authority), which is `ProcessCreate`'s rule again and
/// why this decoder takes two arguments.
///
/// `TierGet` answers a plain fact about the machine. It performs no act, mints
/// no handle, spends no resource, answers no failure status, and hands back a
/// `ProtectionTier` in the value register. It is on System because the tier is
/// a property of the machine rather than of any process, the argument that put
/// `ProcessCreate` and `SlabDonate` here, and it takes no arguments.
///
/// It is an op and not a boot-info field because there is no boot-info carrier
/// to put it in. `sosabi.boot` holds the boot-handle record (a tag, a kind and
/// a handle, exactly the givable kinds) and nothing platform-shaped; delivery
/// is the retrieval op, not a bootinfo section, which is the vDSO discipline's
/// point; and `kernel/sysapi` depends on `sosabi` and `sosrt` only, never on a
/// HAL, so a build-time constant cannot see the tier either.
///
/// `InterruptBind` binds an IRQ line and mints the Interrupt that names it into
/// the caller's table. A line is a machine-wide resource, so the op is on
/// System for `ProcessCreate`'s reason, and a fault it raises lands on the
/// caller. Its one argument is the line. The new handle is charged to the
/// caller's `Handle` row like any mint, but the bind has no Interrupt row: at
/// most one Interrupt is live per line, so the board's line count and the
/// Interrupt slab bound the objects.
///
/// `StatsRegionMap` hands out access without possession. It installs a
/// read-only protection row over the kernel's published stats region in the
/// caller's own domain and answers a `Mapping` naming that row, together with
/// the caller's own row index in the region.
///
/// No `Memory` handle is minted, and that is the whole shape. Every other way
/// to obtain a mapping starts from a Memory or an IoMemory: possession of
/// bytes, then authority over an address space, two rights on two objects. The
/// stats region is neither: it is kernel storage, so a Memory handle onto it
/// would carry `Split` and make it donatable, and neither could mean anything
/// over the page the kernel counts traps into. What the caller is granted is
/// the access and nothing that owns it.
///
/// One right, because there is one object. The two-rights doctrine exists
/// because installing a row in somebody else's address space is a second
/// authority; this op installs into the caller's own and takes no Process
/// handle to name another. So `SystemRight.StatsRegionMap` is the whole gate,
/// and it is on System for `TierGet`'s reason: the region describes the
/// machine's processes rather than any one of them.
///
/// It is `map`'s shape. The `Mapping` handle comes back in the value register
/// and the address comes from `MappingOp.Base`, as for `MemoryOp.Map`: one
/// mechanism for "the address is the kernel's answer". Its arguments are a
/// copy-out buffer and its capacity, which carry back the one thing `map` has
/// no equivalent of: the caller's own row index in the region, without which a
/// caller holds a table it cannot find itself in. See
/// `STATS_REGION_MAP_RECORD_WORDS`.
///
/// `ProcessNext` and `ProcessByPid` observe the machine's processes, both on
/// `SystemRight.ProcessObserve`. Each takes the address of a doubleword in the
/// caller's memory, because a pid is 64 bits and one profile's registers are
/// not: `ProcessNext` reads a cursor (a pid, zero before the first) from it,
/// answers the process with the smallest pid above it and writes that pid back,
/// and `ProcessByPid` reads a pid and answers the process it names. Both answer
/// a Process handle carrying the observe set, capped by the caller's Process
/// ceiling, in the value register; neither takes a keep mask. The walk ends in
/// `Drained` and a pid the record table does not hold is `Revoked`. The kernel
/// keeps no walk state: the cursor is the caller's.
public enum SystemOp: UInt {
    case DebugPrint = 0,
    case Shutdown = 1,
    case ProcessSelf = 2,
    case ClockGet = 3,
    case ProcessCreate = 4,
    case SlabDonate = 5,
    case TierGet = 6,
    case StatsRegionMap = 7,
    case InterruptBind = 8,
    case ProcessNext = 9,
    case ProcessByPid = 10,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Process object's op table (spec §2, §7, §8)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on the Process object. Numbered from zero in its own table:
/// `ThreadCreate` is 0 here and `DebugPrint` is 0 on System, and the pair
/// (handle, op) is what tells them apart.
///
/// `ThreadCreate` lives here rather than on Thread because there is no Thread
/// to name yet, and because spec §7 puts the band map on the Process object,
/// which makes thread creation the place a band would be resolved against it.
/// Thread creation resolves no band; the band map is a loader artifact.
///
/// `Start` is a receiver op, so it keeps the bare verb under the naming rule:
/// the receiver is the process being started, so it is asked of the child's
/// handle and gated on `ProcessRight.Start`. The creation that produced that
/// child is `SystemOp.ProcessCreate`, since a process is a machine-wide
/// resource and only the System object is machine-wide. The two-phase lifecycle
/// is one op on each object.
///
/// The factory ops (`ThreadCreate`, `EventCreate`, `WaiterCreate`,
/// `PipeCreate`) act on behalf of the process the handle names, whether or not
/// that is the caller. That process owns the new object and is charged its
/// quota row; the new handle goes into the caller's table, charged to the
/// caller's `Handle` row, so the word that comes back is the caller's own.
///
/// Op numbers are not ABI (userspace names the case and this module supplies
/// the value, and the two are compiled into one image together), so a table may
/// renumber, and appending is what keeps existing rows where they were.
///
/// `BootHandleNext` is the boot-set iterator: the boot set enters user mode one
/// handle wide (§12), the register carries System, and everything else the
/// kernel minted for root at boot is drained through this op one record at a
/// time. The set it drains is the caller's own, whether the kernel filled it at
/// boot or a launcher filled it with `Give`. The op is self-only: a Process
/// handle naming another process faults the caller (`NotSelf`).
///
/// `Give` is the courier op, and it keeps the bare verb for `Start`'s reason:
/// the receiver is the child. A launcher asks the child's Process handle to
/// accept a capability, tagged with a word it chooses; the kernel moves the
/// handle into a fresh slot of the child's table and answers with a status
/// alone, because the child-side word is irrelevant to the launcher (it can
/// call no op through it, so nothing returns it). What crosses instead is the
/// tag: the giver's own word handed back unread at the child's drain, the
/// `Waiter.add` key precedent. The kernel is a courier, never an interpreter;
/// the launcher and child agree on meaning through config.
///
/// `Give` takes a keep mask as a third argument, on `MINT_OP`'s exact terms:
/// the entry the child receives carries `source & keep`, all-ones is "the
/// rights the giver held" and is what the no-argument spelling means, and a bit
/// the source does not hold is a no-op. The polarity is `MINT_OP`'s, for the
/// same reason: a keep mask fails closed when a kind gains a right, where a
/// removal mask would hand every future authority to every child a launcher had
/// ever configured.
///
/// What the mask saves is a handle row. Attenuating before a give is also
/// possible by minting a masked sibling and giving that, but it costs a table
/// row and a trap for a handle whose only purpose is to be moved out
/// immediately. Naming the mask at the give says the same thing in one op.
/// No-amplification does not move, because an intersection is an intersection
/// wherever it is written.
///
/// `PipeCreate` is the factory for a connection. It sits here rather than on
/// System because pipes are IPC and carry nothing system-shaped. It reads
/// `object_operation` under the naming rule, as `EventCreate` and
/// `WaiterCreate` do, and is gated by `ProcessRight.PipeCreate`.
///
/// It answers with both ends through a copy-out record (two machine words,
/// offsets published beside the wait record's), because one op returns one word
/// in the value register and the copy funnel is the precedented door
/// (`Clock.Now`'s is the two-word-record precedent). Both ends are minted into
/// the caller's table, whichever process the handle names, and wired outward
/// with `give`, so a launcher can hand the inlet to one child and the outlet to
/// another with the launch flow unchanged.
///
/// `Stats` is the introspection op. It answers through a copy-out record, for
/// the reason `PipeCreate` and `Clock.Now` do: one op returns one word and the
/// answer is four 64-bit columns: three counters and the process's pid.
///
/// The counters are split by trap kind because the split is what makes
/// assertions possible. A single "traps" number would be the sum of a
/// deterministic quantity and a timing-dependent one, so no test could assert
/// it; separated, `syscalls` is exact for a given program path (a park is one
/// syscall trap however long it parks) and `interrupts` is the one that moves.
/// `faults` is exact where a test provokes one on purpose. The fourth column,
/// `pid`, is the pid the process's messages carry (spec §2.7). See
/// `PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS` for the layout and the attribution rule.
///
/// `RestrictRights` and `Ceiling` act on the named process's rights ceilings
/// (spec §2.7), one per `HandleKind`. `RestrictRights(kind, mask)` narrows the
/// ceiling for `kind` to its old value intersected with `mask`, answering a
/// status alone; `Ceiling(kind)` answers the ceiling in the value register. A
/// `kind` that is not a `HandleKind` is a `FaultReason.BadArg` fault. Both
/// answer `Revoked` through the handle of a process that has ended, which has
/// no ceilings left to name. `RestrictRights` is gated by
/// `ProcessRight.RestrictRights` and `Ceiling` by `ProcessRight.Stats`.
///
/// `Exit` is self-only: through a handle naming another process it faults the
/// caller (`NotSelf`).
///
/// `Kill` ends the process the handle names, with `arg0` as the code its status
/// word keeps (low 16 bits, under `ProcessStatusKind.Killed`), and answers `Ok`
/// once the teardown is done. It is gated by `ProcessRight.Kill`. A handle
/// naming the caller's own process is a `NotSelf` fault, a `Created` one a
/// `BadState` fault that changes nothing, and a `Gone` one answers `Revoked`
/// with its recorded death unchanged. Killing root stops the machine.
public enum ProcessOp: UInt {
    case ThreadCreate = 0,
    case ThreadSelf = 1,
    case Exit = 2,
    case GetStatus = 3,
    case EventCreate = 4,
    case WaiterCreate = 5,
    case Start = 6,
    case BootHandleNext = 7,
    case Give = 8,
    case PipeCreate = 9,
    case Stats = 10,
    case RestrictRights = 11,
    case Ceiling = 12,
    case Kill = 13,
}

/// Whether `ProcessOp.Start`'s second argument carries a boot tag: the op's
/// first argument, and the thing that makes "no tag" expressible.
///
/// Zero is a legitimate tag, so the absence cannot be encoded in the tag
/// itself. Root's boot regions are tagged with region ordinals starting at 0,
/// so reusing `NO_HANDLE`'s zero would make the very first row of every region
/// table unnameable. A reserved all-ones sentinel is refused for a sharper
/// reason: a tag is the giver's own word handed back unread, so a kernel that
/// reserved one value would be interpreting the vocabulary it exists not to
/// interpret. A flag argument costs one register that `Start` was not using and
/// reserves nothing.
///
/// It is a backed enum rather than a bare 0/1 for the house rule (a closed
/// named set of values is an enum, not a family of statics), which also makes
/// the decode at the trap boundary `from(raw:)`: total, so a process passing 2
/// gets the ordinary `BadArg` fault rather than an index the kernel trusts.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register it arrives in, for the reason
/// `EventMode` and `ClockType` give: the value is process-controlled, so a
/// narrower backing would need a truncation before `from(raw:)` and `0x100`
/// would arrive as a valid `Absent`.
///
/// The cases are `Absent`/`Present` rather than `None`/`Tag` so that nothing in
/// a `match` over one reads like an `Optional`.
///
/// Don't add a `Donate` form (a give performed at the start barrier): a
/// launcher mints a sibling of the child's Process handle, gives the sibling,
/// and starts through the original it still holds. The launch flow is mint,
/// give, start, three ordinary ops, and `Start` does one thing.
///
/// `Present` resolution consumes the record it names. The register is the
/// delivery: the tag is looked up, the word goes into the child's first
/// argument register, and that record is spent, so the drain never answers with
/// a word the child already holds. Otherwise a child that adopted the
/// register's word and took the same record's wrapper would hold two owners of
/// one word, and the second drop would be a fault the program did nothing to
/// deserve.
public enum BootTagForm: UInt {
    case Absent = 0,
    case Present = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Thread object's op table (spec §2, §8)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on the Thread object.
///
/// `Join` is here, which revises spec §8's "no join syscall". The §8 reasoning
/// was that threads are pool workers and work completion is a task-level
/// concept; keeping the object honest instead means a thread you can create and
/// start is a thread whose completion you must be able to observe, and §8
/// already conceded the observation is needed (it proposed thread handles as
/// waitables). A Thread is not a waitable (a Waiter cannot attach one), so
/// `Join` is the only way to wait for one.
public enum ThreadOp: UInt {
    case Start = 0,
    case Join = 1,
    case Exit = 2,
    case Yield = 3,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Event object's op table (spec §2.4)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on an Event object.
///
/// Two ops and the object is complete, which is why §2.4 calls it the smallest
/// thing that can be a kernel object at all: bits go in and never block, and
/// one read takes them all out and resets. There is no third question to ask an
/// Event: "is it ready" is `word != 0`, and it is asked by the Waiter, not by
/// an op.
///
/// `Receive` is the non-blocking door, not the only one: a `Waiter.wait`
/// delivery takes the same word the same way. The two differ in parking, which
/// is why this stayed one op rather than growing a blocking twin.
///
/// There is deliberately no non-draining peek. A drain that could be skipped
/// would make level-triggered readiness something a reader could observe
/// without clearing, so a poll loop could spin forever on a readiness nothing
/// consumes; §2.4's "one drain reads-and-clears" forecloses that, and one op is
/// how it stays foreclosed.
public enum EventOp: UInt {
    case Signal = 0,
    case Receive = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Waiter object's op table (spec §2.2)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on a Waiter object.
///
/// The names are §2.2's own (`add`, `remove`, `wait`); the right that gates the
/// first two is `WaiterRight.Attach`, one capability under a name that says
/// what it is rather than repeating either op.
///
/// `Remove` takes the key, not the waitable's handle. Detaching edits the
/// Waiter's own attachment table and never touches the waitable at all, so the
/// authority it needs is the Waiter handle already in the handle register plus
/// the key that names the attachment; asking for the waitable's handle too
/// would be asking for authority the operation does not spend. It also has to
/// be this way because an attachment outlives the handle it was made through
/// (it holds its own reference, and `Waiter.give` releases the handle at the
/// attach), so a handle-based remove could not name an attachment whose handle
/// is gone.
///
/// Keys are unique per Waiter, and that is an invariant. `Add` with a key the
/// Waiter already uses is a fault (caller-checkable, since the attacher chose
/// both keys), and it has to be refused rather than allowed because a duplicate
/// makes two questions ambiguous at once: which attachment a `Remove` names,
/// and which one a `Wait` answer came from.
public enum WaiterOp: UInt {
    case Add = 0,
    case Remove = 1,
    case Wait = 2,
}

/// `Add`'s third argument: the attachment flags.
///
/// Two bits, and they are orthogonal axes rather than modes. Bit 0 says what
/// happens at delivery (detach, or stay subscribed); bit 1 says what happens to
/// the caller's handle entry at the attach (the op releases it, and the
/// attachment owns the object from then on). That is a 2x2, which is why
/// `OneShotConsuming` is not an enum case.
///
/// The typed surface splits them differently, on purpose. Consuming is an
/// effect change (the wrapper is taken by move and is provably dead at the call
/// site), and a runtime flag can change neither a return type nor a Saw
/// function's effect. So `sos` spells the consuming attach as a distinct verb
/// (`Waiter.give`) while `AttachMode` stays a defaulted value parameter. Here,
/// at the wire, both are bits: the kernel's job is one op, and which of the
/// four combinations it was told is a number.
///
/// A word with any other bit set is a `BadArg` fault: the set is published, so
/// the caller could have checked (§5.7's own line).
public static WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT: UInt = 1 << 0
public static WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING: UInt = 1 << 1

/// Every flag `Add` knows. Anything outside it is outside the op's domain.
public static WAITER_ADD_FLAGS: UInt = WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT | WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING

// =============================================================================
// The Interrupt object's op table (spec §2 table, §9)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on an Interrupt object.
///
/// One op, and the object is complete, for a sharper reason than `EventOp`'s.
/// Everything an Interrupt does to a process happens through a Waiter (§2.2),
/// and everything the kernel does to the line happens in the trap path; what is
/// left for the holder to ask is the one question spec §9 names: this fire is
/// finished, let the device interrupt again.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Wait` op here. Parking is the Waiter's, and a
/// second way to park on one Interrupt would be a second wake protocol to keep
/// in step with the first; §2.2's aggregator exists so that every waitable has
/// exactly one.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Bind`/`Unbind` pair either: the binding is the
/// object's existence (`SystemOp.InterruptBind` takes the line), and releasing
/// its one handle destroys it and gives the line back. A rebindable Interrupt
/// would let one handle name two different devices over its life, which a
/// capability must not do.
public enum InterruptOp: UInt {
    case Ack = 0,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Clock object's op table (spec §2 table)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on a Clock object.
///
/// Two ops: read the time, and make something that fires against it. There is
/// deliberately no set: a Monotonic clock that could be set would not be one,
/// and a domain that can step (Realtime) would bring its own op if it needs
/// one.
///
/// `Now` answers through a copy-out record rather than the value register,
/// because a nanosecond count is 64 bits and one of the two profiles has 32-bit
/// registers. The vDSO true-mapping upgrade (spec §5.7) is the named
/// syscall-free future for this op and changes nothing about its shape.
public enum ClockOp: UInt {
    case Now = 0,
    case TimerCreate = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Timer object's op table (spec §2 table)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on a Timer object.
///
/// Two ops, and no ack, which is the difference from an Interrupt worth reading
/// here. §9's cycle needs an ack because the kernel masks the line and only the
/// driver can say the device has been serviced; a Timer has no device and no
/// mask, so it re-arms itself and a fire is consumed by the wait that reports
/// it. That is the timerfd model, and it makes a periodic timer a
/// fire-and-forget schedule rather than a request/response.
///
/// `Arm` takes its arguments through a copy-in record, because two 64-bit
/// nanosecond counts are four words on a 32-bit profile and the argument
/// registers are three. See `TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS` below.
///
/// `Disarm` is separate from "arm with no deadline" because the two mean
/// different things to the fire count: arming replaces a schedule and clears
/// what the old one owed, while disarming says the timer is off and owes
/// nothing at all. Collapsing them would make "cancel" and "reschedule"
/// indistinguishable at the ABI.
public enum TimerOp: UInt {
    case Arm = 0,
    case Disarm = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Memory object's op table (spec §2.5)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on a Memory object.
///
/// Two ops: derive, and install. They are the whole of what a RAM region is
/// for, and neither reads it. There is no op that hands back a Memory's bounds,
/// so a region stays a capability rather than a description (spec §2.5).
///
/// `Split` cuts `len` bytes out of a region and mints a new Memory over them.
/// On a pool root the cut is served first fit from the root's free list, and
/// only when no free range can hold it, from the front of the root's unspent
/// frontier. On any other region it comes off the front, `[base, base + len)`,
/// and the parent becomes the remainder `[base + len, top)`. Every piece stays
/// representable in the one `{base, len}` slot shape the object already has.
///
/// Arbitrary-offset carving is refused by the shape, not by a check: a cut from
/// the middle would fragment the parent into two remainders, and one slot
/// cannot hold two ranges. The restriction is not a rule somebody could relax
/// without noticing; it is what the representation permits.
///
/// It is exclusive, which is the difference from `IoMemoryOp.Carve`: what a
/// split hands out, the parent no longer has. A region is freed when no handle
/// names it and no installed row maps it, and a pool root also waits until
/// none of its bytes are on loan. A freed piece's range returns to its pool
/// root's free list; a freed root's range has no pool to return to, and its
/// free-list nodes go back to their slab. A range that needs a free-list node
/// when none is left is dropped from the pool and counted.
///
/// `Map` installs the region in a domain and answers with a Mapping handle to
/// the caller. Its arguments are a target Process handle and the access bits
/// (`MapAccess`), and it spends two rights on two objects, `MemoryRight.Map`
/// here and `ProcessRight.Map` on the target, because possession of bytes must
/// not imply authority over an address space.
///
/// An access word naming `MapAccess.Execute` also spends
/// `MemoryRight.MapExecute` on this region. It is conditional because it is a
/// bit about what is being asked for rather than about the op: a caller mapping
/// RW spends exactly what it always did.
public enum MemoryOp: UInt {
    case Split = 0,
    case Map = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The IoMemory object's op table (spec §2.5)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on an IoMemory object.
///
/// The same two shapes `MemoryOp` has, with the derivation verb changed, and
/// the change is the kind's whole reason for existing.
///
/// `Carve` leaves the parent alone: `carve(offset, len)` mints a sub-window
/// over `[base + offset, base + offset + len)` and the parent is unchanged.
/// That is §2.5's "a fixed region may be handed out many times": MMIO is
/// pinned, so there is nothing to consume and several drivers may hold windows
/// onto one peripheral.
///
/// The hardware's own granularity is checked at the carve, against a
/// per-profile HAL predicate, and violating it is a `BadArg` fault because the
/// caller chose both numbers. One profile expresses a device window as a single
/// naturally-aligned power-of-two protection entry, so `len` must be a power of
/// two and the base a multiple of it; the other grants by page.
///
/// It is checked at the carve because that is where a caller chose the numbers,
/// and a window that could never be installed anywhere is a capability that
/// lies about itself; a refusal deferred to the map would arrive one op later
/// than the mistake. `Map` checks it again because of the one window a carve
/// never saw: a boot root, whose bounds came from the build's region table
/// rather than from any caller. Two checks, two different things being
/// distrusted.
///
/// `Map` installs a device-attribute row, always, which is the type-level
/// guarantee `ObjType.IoMemory` exists for: there is no other op on this kind,
/// so a cacheable view of a register block is unreachable by construction.
/// Access is RW and fixed: execute-on-device is refused vocabulary (registers
/// are not instructions), and a write-only register block is not a grant either
/// profile can express.
public enum IoMemoryOp: UInt {
    case Carve = 0,
    case Map = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Mapping object's op table (spec §2.5)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on a Mapping object.
///
/// Two ops. `Unmap` is the thing a Mapping is for (§2.5 gives it exactly one
/// job: it is the thing that can unmap), and `Base` reads where the row landed.
/// Everything else about it, such as what access it carries, was decided when
/// it was installed and cannot be edited afterwards. A mapping whose access
/// should change is a mapping that is unmapped and made again, which is one
/// fewer op and no ambiguity about what a half-changed row means.
///
/// Unmap of an already-unmapped Mapping is `BadState`, caller-checkable since
/// the caller is the one that unmapped it. The same tag covers a mapping whose
/// target process has died: its row went with the domain, so there is nothing
/// left to remove, and "this object is not in a state to do that" is exactly
/// what happened.
public enum MappingOp: UInt {
    case Unmap = 0,
    /// Where this row landed in its target's address space.
    ///
    /// Once the kernel chooses a user address, a program that maps a region has
    /// no other way to learn where it is: the map answers with a capability, a
    /// `Memory` has no bounds reader by design, and a build-time constant is a
    /// number about the frames rather than about the address space.
    ///
    /// It is on the Mapping and not on the map's return for the reason §2.5
    /// gives the object at all: a Mapping owns its row, so the row's address is
    /// this object's own property, minted with it and revoked with it. A holder
    /// that is not the target (a launcher that mapped into a child) learns
    /// where the child sees it, which is information about a domain it already
    /// had the authority to edit.
    ///
    /// The answer is a user address on every tier. On a tier that does not
    /// translate it is also the physical one, which is that tier's answer to
    /// the same question rather than a different question.
    case Base = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The PipeInlet and PipeOutlet op tables (spec §2.1)
// =============================================================================

/// A method id on a PipeInlet, the client end.
///
/// `post` is the non-blocking client primitive: stage the message, never
/// suspend, answer. `send(timeout:)`, tell and cancel are library compositions
/// over it plus the Timer and Waiter machinery, so the kernel never learns
/// what a timeout is. `Call`, below, is the one other submission door.
///
/// It answers the claim: the value register carries a `PipeReply` handle minted
/// into the poster's table. A refused post mints nothing, so
/// `SosStatus.WouldBlock`, `SosStatus.PeerClosed` and a handle-table refusal
/// for the claim (`NoResource`, `QuotaExceeded`) leave the value register
/// meaningless. Both ride the typed tier's error channel: `post` answers
/// `Ok(claim)` or fails, and a caller that genuinely polls writes a
/// `WouldBlock` arm. No wire value depends on that.
///
/// Its arguments are a `PipeSendRecord`: (record address, record bytes) through
/// the copy-in funnel `Timer.Arm` opened. A message carries handles, so the
/// body's address, its length and the handle words travel together in one
/// record. A length of zero is legal, because §2.1 makes a data-free message an
/// option, and so is a record whose handle slots are all `NO_HANDLE`, which is
/// what an ordinary data-only post looks like.
///
/// `Call` is `post`, the park and the resolve run without returning to
/// userspace. It is a performance amendment and not a semantic one (every rule
/// the composed path obeys applies verbatim), so the two ops sit in one table
/// rather than one replacing the other: `post` is still the primitive a
/// multiplexer builds on, and `Call` is the fused shape of the blocking RPC
/// that dominates.
public enum PipeInletOp: UInt {
    case Post = 0,
    /// Post, park, and resolve behind one trap.
    ///
    /// Its arguments are (send record, record bytes, reply record buffer) and
    /// its value is the reply's length. The reply is a `PipeMsgRecord`, because
    /// a reply carries handles on exactly the terms a request does (§2.1's
    /// `send(data?, handles?)` is symmetric), so the destination is the same
    /// delivered-message layout a `Take` writes. It is gated by
    /// `PipeInletRight.Post` and by nothing else: a `Call` submits a message
    /// exactly as a `post` does, and the fusion is mechanics rather than
    /// authority. The park needs no `Wait` right because no Waiter is involved:
    /// the claim is kernel-internal, tied to the parked thread, consumed at the
    /// reply, so a fused call mints no `PipeReply` into the caller's table and
    /// spends no handle row.
    ///
    /// It never answers `WouldBlock`: a full ring parks on the inlet's
    /// room-to-post level exactly as the composed send does, and the poster
    /// resumes when an exchange settles. `PeerClosed`, before or during, is
    /// what a dropped obligation and a dead server end come back as. The one
    /// other refusal is a reply whose handles find no room in the caller's
    /// table (`NoResource`, `QuotaExceeded`): the parked caller cannot ask
    /// again, so the reply and its handles are lost.
    ///
    /// There is no duration argument: the kernel never learns what a timeout
    /// is. A caller that wants one composes `post` + `wait` + a Timer.
    ///
    /// The reply buffer carries no capacity: the third argument register is
    /// spent on the buffer's address, so the buffer is checked against
    /// `pipe_msg_record_bytes()` through the copy door and against the caller's
    /// real memory, a stronger test than a number the caller typed.
    case Call = 1,
}

/// A method id on a PipeOutlet, the server end.
///
/// `take` is nonblocking: it copies the oldest staged message out through the
/// funnel, and an empty ring is `SosStatus.WouldBlock`, never a park. A server
/// that wants to wait attaches the outlet to a Waiter.
///
/// It answers two things: the message, and the `PipeRequest` that obliges its
/// reply, minted into the taker's table at the take. One op answers one word,
/// so the two travel in a copy-out record (`PipeTakeRecord`, in `records`), as
/// `ProcessOp.PipeCreate`'s two ends do.
///
/// The message is in that record. Its arguments are (record, record capacity)
/// and nothing else: a message carries handles, so a separate body buffer would
/// become a body buffer plus a handle buffer plus a kind buffer.
/// `PipeTakeRecord` is the obligation, the packed meta, the handle words and
/// the body in one fixed-prefix layout, copied out as one contiguous prefix
/// ending at the message's length, so a short message pays for no part of the
/// body region.
public enum PipeOutletOp: UInt {
    case Take = 0,
}

/// A method id on a PipeReply, the client's claim on one reply (spec §2.1).
///
/// Two ops, and they split the act from the question. `Resolve` is the act: it
/// consumes the claim's entry on every path it can return through, and parks
/// the calling thread when the answer has not been written yet. `Ready` is the
/// question: the same level asked non-blockingly, consuming nothing.
///
/// `Resolve` never answers `WouldBlock`. It answers the reply bytes (consuming
/// the entry with them), `SosStatus.PeerClosed` (consuming it too), or, when
/// the caller's table has no room for the handles the reply carries,
/// `NoResource` or `QuotaExceeded` (consuming it as well). Where the answer is
/// not there yet it parks on the claim's ready level and finishes on the wake.
/// A park is mechanics rather than authority, so the op spends the existing
/// `PipeReplyRight.Resolve` bit and nothing new. Because a claim is spent on
/// every path, the typed tier can make it a move-taking (`consumes`) method, so
/// a second use is a compile error rather than a `BadHandle` fault.
///
/// The blocking `send(msg)` / `send(msg, timeout:)` compositions are built on
/// top of the wait record and do not use `Resolve`: the reply is in the wait
/// record, so a client that attaches its claim never resolves at all. A claim
/// whose answer was delivered through a wait answers `PeerClosed` here; there
/// is no second copy.
///
/// `Resolve`'s arguments are (record, capacity) and its value is the reply's
/// length. The record is a `PipeMsgRecord`, `PipeOutletOp.Take`'s own shape
/// reused on the way back, which is what §2.1's symmetric `send(data?,
/// handles?)` asks for: a reply may carry handles, so the consuming door has to
/// be able to hand them over too.
public enum PipeReplyOp: UInt {
    case Resolve = 0,
    /// Has the answer arrived? The level, asked without spending anything.
    ///
    /// Status-only: no record, no copy-out, no value. `Ok`: the answer is
    /// written and a `Resolve` will return it without parking.
    /// `SosStatus.WouldBlock`: not yet, and the exchange is still live.
    /// `SosStatus.PeerClosed`: it can never arrive, so the holder drops the
    /// claim rather than spending a resolve on a dead exchange. Readiness is
    /// monotonic (pending goes to settled and never back), so
    /// ready-then-resolve has no check-then-act race.
    ///
    /// It is gated on `PipeReplyRight.Wait`, not on `Resolve`. The bit that
    /// gates the level question is the bit that gates attaching the claim to a
    /// Waiter, because they are the same question asked two ways; `Resolve`
    /// stays the consuming act's authority, so a claim narrowed to `Wait` alone
    /// can be watched and never spent.
    ///
    /// It consumes nothing, including on the terminal arm. A `PeerClosed` here
    /// leaves the claim alive and the caller drops it, which is the point of
    /// putting the terminal on the error channel: no second call for a reply
    /// that is never coming.
    case Ready = 1,
}

/// A method id on a PipeRequest, the server's obligation (spec §2.1).
///
/// `reply` discharges the obligation either way: it copies the reply body in
/// and consumes the request. To an abandoned request (the client dropped its
/// claim) it answers `SosStatus.PeerClosed` and the obligation is discharged
/// all the same, which is §2.1's answer: never a silent success, ignorable with
/// `let _ =` on the std-Channel close precedent.
///
/// Its arguments are a `PipeSendRecord`, `PipeInletOp.Post`'s shape mirrored. A
/// length of zero is legal here too, because a reply that carries no data is
/// still an answer, and so is a reply that carries nothing but handles. The
/// request handle the op is invoked through is consumed, so a record that
/// carries it is a `BadArg` fault on the caller; `ReplyWait` has the same rule,
/// and also refuses a record that carries the Waiter word its wait leg parks
/// on.
///
/// `ReplyWait` is a reply with a built-in wait: the server's half of the fused
/// pair, and the mirror of `PipeInletOp.Call`. It lives on the request because
/// the reply is the primary act and it is what consumes the receiver, so the op
/// belongs to the object being consumed and the wait is the fused tail. There
/// is no degenerate form and no optional waiter: the first turn of a server
/// loop is the existing `WaiterOp.Wait`, the dead-client recovery is that same
/// call, and reply-without-wait is `Reply`. An optional waiter would change the
/// return type on a runtime value, which the tree refuses.
public enum PipeRequestOp: UInt {
    case Reply = 0,
    /// Discharge this obligation, then park on a waiter, behind one trap.
    ///
    /// Its arguments are (args record, args record bytes, wait record buffer)
    /// and it is gated by `PipeRequestRight.Reply` here plus the waiter's own
    /// `WaiterRight.Wait`; both are checked, because the op does both things.
    /// The args record is a `PipeReplyWaitRecord`: a waiter handle in front of
    /// the same `PipeSendRecord` every other sending op takes. The reply body
    /// could not ride the registers beside a wait buffer, so the small words
    /// travel through the copy-in funnel `TimerOp.Arm` opened and the body
    /// stays an address, which keeps the wrapper from copying the body itself.
    ///
    /// The request is consumed on every path (§2.1, and the transfer-funnel
    /// contract): a refused reply discharges the obligation exactly as `Reply`
    /// does, and the op then returns without parking so that one dead client
    /// can never stall a server loop.
    ///
    /// The answer has two channels and the value register is the leg
    /// (`ReplyWaitLeg`): `Ok` is a delivered wait record, and a failure carries
    /// the status plus which half of the op produced it. `Wait` is zero because
    /// every outcome after the park is a wait-leg outcome, and every waker in
    /// the kernel already writes a zero value register, so only the reply leg
    /// has to say anything.
    case ReplyWait = 1,
    /// Mint a Process handle onto the process that sent the request this
    /// obligation answers (spec §2.1). Takes no argument and answers the
    /// handle in the value register.
    ///
    /// Gated by `PipeRequestRight.Sender`. The handle carries the observe set
    /// (`process_observe_rights()`) capped by the caller's Process ceiling, and
    /// is charged to the caller's `Handle` row. A sender that has died answers
    /// `Revoked` and mints nothing: the exchange keeps the sender's pid and
    /// record index and no reference, and the op accepts the record only if
    /// it is live and still holds that pid. The obligation is not consumed and
    /// the reply is still owed.
    case Sender = 2,
}

/// Which half of a `PipeRequestOp.ReplyWait` produced the answer.
///
/// It rides the value register beside the status, and `Wait = 0` is
/// load-bearing rather than arbitrary: a `ReplyWait` that gets past its reply
/// leg parks, and every path that answers a parked thread (a delivery, a
/// revocation, a teardown) writes a zero value register already. So the wait
/// leg needs no cooperation from any waker, and the reply leg, the only outcome
/// that returns without parking, is the one that stamps a number.
///
/// `Wait(_)` therefore says the reply landed, which is the distinction the
/// server loop is written against: a `Reply` leg failure means recover by
/// waiting afresh, and a `Wait` leg failure means the exchange is complete and
/// the wait is what failed.
public enum ReplyWaitLeg: UInt {
    case Wait = 0,
    case Reply = 1,
}

// Every op table is dense from zero, and stays below the universal ops. That is
// what makes a universal op number safe: it can never collide with a per-kind
// op, because no kind is allowed to reach it. The bound tested is `MINT_OP`,
// the lowest of the three, so one assert per table covers all, and adding
// another universal op below it tightens every one of these at once. There is
// one assert per table, on its highest case, in the spirit of the rights
// asserts above: the repetition is the check, and a table growing toward the
// top of the space fails to build rather than failing as a universal op that
// quietly ran somebody's op.
//
// When an op is added to a table, re-point that table's assert at the new
// highest case. A bound tested against a case that is no longer the highest
// passes while the table walks past `INFO_OP`.
static_assert((SystemOp.ProcessByPid as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((ProcessOp.Kill as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((ThreadOp.Yield as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((EventOp.Receive as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((WaiterOp.Wait as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((InterruptOp.Ack as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((ClockOp.TimerCreate as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((TimerOp.Disarm as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((MemoryOp.Map as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((IoMemoryOp.Map as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
// Re-point this assert at the highest case when an op is added (see the note on
// the System table's assert above).
static_assert((MappingOp.Base as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((PipeInletOp.Call as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((PipeOutletOp.Take as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((PipeReplyOp.Ready as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")
static_assert((PipeRequestOp.ReplyWait as UInt) < INFO_OP,
              "an op table must stay below the universal ops")

// ...and the universal numbers are distinct and ordered, which is the fact the
// asserts above lean on when they test the lowest one.
static_assert(INFO_OP < MINT_OP,
              "the universal ops descend from the top of the number space")
static_assert(MINT_OP < RELEASE_OP,
              "the universal ops descend from the top of the number space")

/// Which clock a Timer runs against, and what `System.ClockGet` names (§2's
/// Clock row).
///
/// `Monotonic` is the only domain declared. `Boot` and `Realtime` are named as
/// future domains and are deliberately not declared: an unproducible case is
/// dead surface, and a caller able to name one the kernel cannot mint would get
/// a runtime refusal where the absence is a compile error. The raw backing lets
/// them be added without renumbering.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the domain arrives in, for the
/// reason `SystemOp` and `EventMode` give: the value is process-controlled, so
/// a narrower backing would need a truncation before `from(raw:)` and `0x100`
/// would arrive as a valid `Monotonic`.
///
/// Monotonic's contract: nanoseconds since the machine started counting, never
/// stepping and never going backwards. The epoch is not published and must not
/// be relied on: two readings are comparable, one reading means nothing on its
/// own.
public enum ClockType: UInt {
    case Monotonic = 0,
}

/// What the platform can deny: the tier word `SystemOp.TierGet` answers.
///
/// Two words, by design: MMU versus MPU is not advertised. Slot pricing and
/// `map`'s answer are already the visible difference between those two, and a
/// third word would invite tier-sniffing, a program branching on the mechanism
/// instead of on what the mechanism can promise. The question this enum answers
/// is exactly one: can the platform deny?
///
/// `Isolated`: the hardware can refuse an access. `unmap` revokes, neither a
/// peer nor the process itself can reach memory it was not granted (the
/// kernel's included), and the isolation proofs mean something.
///
/// `Flat`: user mode can read and write any address, so the important regions
/// are reached by everyone. What this disclaims is precise, and it is not
/// `map`. `map`'s contract is "make this region accessible with at least these
/// rights", which on flat hardware is trivially true, so the call succeeds
/// honestly rather than being ignored. What is disclaimed is denial: `unmap`'s
/// revocation half, isolation between peers, and protecting the kernel's own
/// memory from a process.
///
/// Handles, rights, quotas and lifetimes are checked identically on the
/// syscall path at every tier, which is why the object-model cases run
/// unchanged on every profile (the flat profile excludes, by name, only cases
/// that assert a hardware denial). On `Flat` they hold only for a process that
/// reaches the kernel through syscalls: one that writes kernel memory directly
/// bypasses all of them, `SystemRight.Shutdown` included. The isolated tiers
/// enforce both the syscall checks and the memory boundary.
///
/// A program that relies on isolation checks this once, at startup, and refuses
/// to run when it reads `Flat`, which is the reason the word exists. Don't make
/// `unmap` return `Ok` on hardware that revoked nothing: a per-call lie is the
/// alternative this word replaces.
///
/// Backed by `UInt` for `SystemOp`'s and `ClockType`'s reason (it rides the
/// value register), though this one travels outward, so the width is about the
/// register rather than about a hostile caller. Both cases are producible by
/// some build, which is what makes declaring both honest where `ClockType`
/// declares one.
public enum ProtectionTier: UInt {
    case Isolated = 0,
    case Flat = 1,
}

/// What a mapping permits: the access bits `MemoryOp.Map` takes (spec §2.5's
/// `map(access:)`).
///
/// Access is a property of the mapping, not of the region, and this enum is
/// where that decision becomes API. `MemoryRight` deliberately has no R/W/X
/// pair (its own docstring says why), so the only place a process names what it
/// may do with memory is here. That makes double-mapping one region read-only
/// in one place and read-write in another expressible. A double map is allowed:
/// two rows and two Mappings, with no aliasing bookkeeping owed.
///
/// It is a flag set, so the values are bits and a combination is the backing
/// integer; Saw ships no OptionSet and an enum is a set of tags.
/// `MapAccess.Read | MapAccess.Write` folds to `3` in any fixed-width slot,
/// which is what the funnels take.
///
/// The numbers are the `SegFlag` numbers: R = 1, W = 2, X = 4 is the order one
/// profile's protection config uses natively, so an image's declared
/// permissions and a caller's requested access are the same vocabulary the
/// whole way down. The kernel still translates rather than casting, in one
/// small function beside the map op, because the two enums live in two packages
/// that must be free to version apart.
///
/// Three things are refused, all caller-checkable `BadArg` faults: write
/// without read (not a grant any target should install, and one profile
/// reserves the encoding outright), no access at all (a row that permits
/// nothing spends a protection slot to say no, which default-deny already
/// says), and write and execute in one row.
///
/// W^X is per row, and it costs nothing expressible. Double-mapping is
/// sanctioned, so the JIT-shaped pattern (an RW row here, an RX row there, over
/// one region) remains available in full while every individual protection row
/// is W^X. What the refusal removes is the one shape that has no honest use: a
/// single row a process can write to and then fetch from. It joins
/// write-without-read rather than being a different kind of check, because both
/// are grants the caller assembled out of cases it named, and both are answered
/// before anything is installed.
///
/// The boundary is the dynamic map. Neither this rule nor
/// `MemoryRight.MapExecute` governs a process image's own executable segments:
/// those arrive through `process_create`'s loader as `SegFlag` bits read out of
/// the image, under `SystemRight.ProcessCreate` authority, and are validated by
/// `imgformat.has_sane_perms` (which refuses write-without-read and
/// X-on-device, and deliberately not W|X, since an image's linker script is
/// what keeps its segments apart). A driver child holds no `ProcessCreate`, so
/// the two doors are not alternatives for it.
///
/// Backed by `UInt32` rather than the register's `UInt`, the one place this
/// file's usual reasoning inverts: a mask is not a decoded number. It never
/// reaches `from(raw:)` (the kernel tests bits and refuses anything outside the
/// three), so there is no truncation hazard, and a fixed-width backing is what
/// makes `MapAccess.Read | MapAccess.Write` fold at an ordinary annotated slot
/// (constant folding reaches fixed-width slots and not platform ones).
/// `SystemRight`'s keep mask is the precedent: both are bit sets a caller
/// assembles.
public enum MapAccess: UInt32 {
    case Read = 1 << 0,
    case Write = 1 << 1,
    case Execute = 1 << 2,
}

/// Every bit `MapAccess` defines: what a caller's access word may contain.
///
/// Named rather than written so the kernel's "these bits and no others" check
/// and this declaration cannot drift: adding a case here is what widens the
/// check.
public static MAP_ACCESS_MASK: UInt32 = ((MapAccess.Read as UInt32)
                                         | (MapAccess.Write as UInt32)
                                         | (MapAccess.Execute as UInt32))

/// How an Event accumulates: a per-Event property, chosen at creation and fixed
/// for its life (§2.4).
///
/// It is fixed rather than settable because the accumulated word means two
/// different things under the two modes, and a mode change with a non-zero word
/// would have to either reinterpret it or discard it. Creating a second Event
/// costs one slab slot.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the mode arrives in, for the
/// reason `SystemOp` gives: the value is process-controlled, so a narrower
/// backing would need a truncation before `from(raw:)` and `0x100` would arrive
/// as a valid `Or`.
public enum EventMode: UInt {
    case Or = 0,
    case SaturatingSum = 1,
}

/// What `INFO_OP` is asked about the handle: the selector in `arg0`.
///
/// Each case is one fact and the op answers that one value. Backed by `UInt`,
/// the width of the register the selector arrives in, for the reason `SystemOp`
/// gives: the value is process-controlled, so the decode is exact and
/// `from(raw:)` rejects every number that is not a selector, which the kernel
/// answers with a `FaultReason.BadArg` fault.
public enum HandleInfo: UInt {
    /// The handle's rights word, read against the rights enum of its kind.
    case Rights = 0,
    /// The handle's kind, as a `HandleKind` number.
    case Kind = 1,
}

/// What kind of object a handle names: the answer to `HandleInfo.Kind`.
///
/// Public API rather than kernel bookkeeping: the kernel writes this value and
/// userspace reads it. It is deliberately not `ObjType`, which is
/// kernel-internal and renumberable: this value crosses the trap boundary, so
/// it is its own closed set with its own stated numbers, and the kernel's
/// `handle_kind_of` is the one place the two enums meet. Unlike
/// `BootHandleKind` it covers every kind a handle can name, including the ones
/// that cannot be given (`Clock`, `Mapping`). A handle that names nothing has
/// no kind: it is a `BadHandle` fault before this is answered.
///
/// A C caller of `sos_handle_info` compares the answer against these numbers.
/// The typed tier needs none of this, since each wrapper knows its kind.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the word it travels in, so a decode through
/// `from(raw:)` is total.
public enum HandleKind: UInt {
    case System = 0,
    case Process = 1,
    case Thread = 2,
    case Event = 3,
    case Waiter = 4,
    case Interrupt = 5,
    case Clock = 6,
    case Timer = 7,
    case Memory = 8,
    case IoMemory = 9,
    case Mapping = 10,
    case PipeInlet = 11,
    case PipeOutlet = 12,
    case PipeReply = 13,
    case PipeRequest = 14,
}

/// How many `HandleKind` cases there are: the length of a process's ceiling
/// table, which is indexed by the kind's number. Kernel-internal; userspace
/// names a kind through the typed overloads or the cases themselves.
public static HANDLE_KINDS: Int = 15

static_assert((HandleKind.PipeRequest as UInt) == ((HANDLE_KINDS - 1) as UInt),
              "HANDLE_KINDS must be one past the highest HandleKind")

/// Which slab a `SystemOp.SlabDonate` is feeding.
///
/// A small dedicated enum rather than `ObjType`. `ObjType` names every kind a
/// handle can name, and the two sets do not coincide in either direction. It
/// has members that are not donatable: `Clock`, which is a census of domains
/// rather than a slab and has nothing to run out of, and the pipe kinds, which
/// are two counted columns over one connection slot, so donating "an inlet" is
/// not a thing to ask for. And the most important donatable slab in the system
/// has no `ObjType` at all: the free-range nodes the allocator's free lists are
/// made of are not objects and have no handles. Reusing `ObjType` would mean a
/// vocabulary that is both too wide and too narrow, with a `from(raw:)` that
/// accepts numbers the kernel then has to refuse one by one. This enum names
/// exactly the donatable slabs, so a value that decodes is a value that can be
/// served.
///
/// The names are plural, deliberately: a `SlabKind` names a table, not an
/// object. `SlabKind.Events` is the Event slab, and the reading "one event" is
/// never available to confuse it with `ObjType.Event`.
///
/// A kind is absent here if and only if it is not a slab, or names one end of a
/// slot two kinds share. Growing a slot array and leaving a satellite behind
/// would be a memory-safety bug rather than a small capacity lie, so a kind
/// whose slot has satellite arrays is either converted (the satellite becomes
/// part of the slot, or a chain the same boundary walk answers) or excluded by
/// name.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the kind arrives in, for the
/// reason `SystemOp` and `EventMode` give: the value is process-controlled, so
/// the decode is exact and `from(raw:)` rejects every number that is not a
/// kind.
public enum SlabKind: UInt {
    case Events = 0,
    case Waiters = 1,
    case Interrupts = 2,
    case Attachments = 3,
    case Timers = 4,
    case Memories = 5,
    case IoMemories = 6,
    case Mappings = 7,
    /// The allocator's free-list nodes: the one donatable slab that backs no
    /// object kind at all.
    case FreeRanges = 8,
    /// Threads, and the frame arena that comes with them. One donation buys
    /// both: a thread is a slot plus a saved context, and the kernel carves the
    /// region into the two arrays itself rather than asking a caller to donate
    /// twice and get the ratio right.
    ///
    /// The context-switch path reads a frame by index, so this kind's
    /// conversion is a reviewed surface; `kcore.threads.frame_slot` carries the
    /// audit.
    case Threads = 9,
    /// Pipe connections, and the `PIPE_INFLIGHT` exchanges each one owns. The
    /// same one-donation-two-arrays shape as `Threads`, with a fan-out: `k`
    /// connections need `k * PIPE_INFLIGHT` exchange records, and a caller
    /// never names either number.
    ///
    /// It is by far the most expensive kind per slot, because a connection
    /// carries its whole staging ring: a donated region buys `len /
    /// (sizeof(PipeSlot) + PIPE_INFLIGHT * sizeof(Exchange))` connections. That
    /// is a fact about pipes rather than about this op, and it is why a
    /// deployment that wants many connections donates a large region.
    case Pipes = 10,
    /// Process bodies: what a live process runs in (spec §2.7).
    ///
    /// The handle table, the boot-handle set with its count and cursor, and the
    /// grant record are fields of `ProcessBody`, not parallel arrays, so a
    /// donated body never indexes off the end of anything.
    ///
    /// One donation, one array. A `ProcessBody` is large (it carries a handle
    /// table, a boot set and a grant record) and entirely plain, so this kind
    /// needs none of `Threads`' side arena or `Pipes`' second chain: a region
    /// buys `len / sizeof(ProcessBody)` bodies and nothing else. It raises how
    /// many processes can be alive at once, and not how many dead ones can be
    /// held (`ProcessRecords`).
    ///
    /// A translating tier has a second ceiling this op cannot move: a
    /// per-process protection domain comes out of a static HAL pool (donation
    /// out of HAL-internal storage is not offered), so `hal.PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS`
    /// bounds what a donated body can actually be used for there. A create past
    /// it answers `NoResource`, the machine's real answer on that tier,
    /// advertised rather than faulted. See
    /// `kcore.process.process_slots_usable`.
    case Processes = 11,
    /// Process records: the process as handles, attachments and charged
    /// objects name it (spec §2.7). The state, the status word, the quota
    /// ledger and the final trap counters are fields of `ProcessRecord`.
    ///
    /// A record outlives its body for as long as a handle or an attachment
    /// names it, so this is the kind a supervisor that keeps its dead
    /// children's handles donates to: it raises how many dead processes can be
    /// held before a create is refused, and on a tier whose domain pool caps
    /// the bodies it still grows. One donation, one array: a region buys
    /// `len / sizeof(ProcessRecord)` records.
    case ProcessRecords = 12,
}

/// The message body's maximum, as a build define (spec §2.1's fixed body).
///
/// It is the size of one staging slot: the largest `len` a `PipeInletOp.Post`
/// may name, and the size of the body region every delivered-message record
/// (`PipeMsgRecord`, `PipeTakeRecord`) and every wait record buffer carries.
/// They are one number because they are one fixed slot seen from several sides.
///
/// §2.1 ratifies a fixed body maximum without fixing the number, so this is the
/// tree's choice within it: large enough for a protocol header plus a small
/// payload, small enough that `PIPE_INFLIGHT` slots per connection stay a
/// zerofill array rather than a budget anybody has to think about. The assert
/// below pins the range the ratified sentence implies: a body must hold at
/// least a machine word, and must stay small enough that one bounded copy is
/// still a bounded copy.
///
/// It is declared here, ahead of the wait record in `records`, because the
/// record names it; keep it above anything an array length reads, since a
/// statics initializer folds in declaration order.
public static PIPE_BODY_BYTES: Int = 128

static_assert(PIPE_BODY_BYTES >= 8,
              "a message body must hold at least one 64-bit word")
static_assert(PIPE_BODY_BYTES <= 1024,
              "a message body is copied with interrupts masked — keep it bounded")
static_assert(PIPE_BODY_BYTES % 8 == 0,
              "a staging slot must be doubleword-sized on both profiles")

/// Handles one message may carry, as a build define (spec §2.1's `send(data?,
/// handles?)`).
///
/// It is here and not in `kcore.limits` because it is part of the contract
/// between the two halves, as `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` is. A ring's depth is a slab
/// dimension nothing outside the kernel needs (a caller meeting
/// a full ring is told `WouldBlock` and never has to know the number), while
/// this is a size the two halves must agree on: the number of handle words a
/// `Post` may name, the number of slots a delivery record carries, and the
/// length of the `[PipeHandle?; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]` a typed caller declares. A
/// caller cannot even spell the array without it.
///
/// The asserts pin the range §2.1 implies: a message that could carry no handle
/// at all would delete the delegation primitive, and the slots are copied
/// inside one bounded rendezvous, so the count stays small enough that the copy
/// is still a bounded copy.
public static PIPE_MSG_HANDLES: Int = 4

static_assert(PIPE_MSG_HANDLES >= 1,
              "a message must be able to carry at least one handle")
static_assert(PIPE_MSG_HANDLES <= 8,
              "a message's handles move inside one rendezvous — keep the count bounded")

// The one range the packed meta word rests on: a delivery's body length rides a
// single byte beside the kind bytes rather than spending a machine word of its
// own, which is only honest while the longest body a message can carry still
// fits one byte (`PIPE_BODY_BYTES` must not exceed `UInt8.max`).
static_assert(PIPE_BODY_BYTES <= (UInt8.max as Int),
              "a message body's length rides one byte of the packed meta word")

/// What a message's handle slots carry beside their words: the packed meta.
///
/// A handle word does not say what it names, and the receiver has to know. A
/// word is `(generation << HANDLE_INDEX_BITS) | (index + 1)` and carries no
/// kind. Every other handle crossing in the system either implies the kind from
/// the op (`Post` answers a claim, `Take` answers an obligation, `mint` answers
/// its source's kind) or transmits it, as the boot-handle record does with its
/// `BootHandleKind` word. A message's slots are the first place the kind is
/// genuinely variable, so the kinds travel.
///
/// They are bytes, and the length packs in with them. A `BootHandleKind` is a
/// small closed set and a body length is at most `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`, so
/// everything a delivery has to say about its variable tail fits in a few
/// bytes, where a standalone `body_len` word would spend a whole machine word
/// to carry a number that fits in one byte. Packing them keeps the fixed prefix
/// small.
///
/// Zero is the empty slot, at every position. `BootHandleKind` has no
/// zero-valued spelling for "nothing" and does not need one, because a slot's
/// kind byte and its handle word are zero together, the same
/// `NO_HANDLE`-is-not-an-index rule the handle table runs on. The kinds are
/// what a decode reads first: a slot whose kind byte is zero is `None` and its
/// word is never looked at, which lets a delivery that carries no handles leave
/// those words unwritten.
///
/// It is a struct rather than a packed integer, for `WaitBuffer`'s reason:
/// declaration order is the layout, so the field names here are the wire
/// description and there is no shift-and-mask arithmetic for the two halves to
/// disagree about. It has alignment one, so it sits inside whatever padding the
/// word-aligned region after it needs.
public struct PipeMsgMeta {
    /// How many of the body's bytes are real. Zero is a data-free message,
    /// which §2.1 makes an option rather than an absence.
    public len: UInt8,
    /// What each message slot names: a `BootHandleKind` raw value, or 0 for an
    /// empty slot.
    public kinds: [UInt8; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
}

static_assert(sizeof<PipeMsgMeta>() == 1 + PIPE_MSG_HANDLES,
              "the packed meta is one length byte and one kind byte per slot")
